var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"functions/#Worksheet-functions","page":"Worksheet functions","text":"These functions can be used in worksheet formulas. All of them except JuliaHandleInfo can also be called from VBA, as for example jl.JuliaCall(...) - see Calling from VBA. For the functions that can only be called from VBA, see VBA functions.\n\nName Description\nJuliaLaunch Launches a local Julia session which listens to the current Excel session and responds to calls to JuliaEval etc.\nJuliaCall Call a named Julia function, passing in data from the worksheet. Returns an error string for results that cannot be displayed on a worksheet (nested arrays, dictionaries etc.). JuliaCallVBA lifts those restrictions.\nJuliaCallNamedArgs Call a named Julia function, passing positional and named arguments from the worksheet. The argument \";\" marks where the named arguments start, given as name-value pairs. A named argument whose value is \";\" must be the last pair.\nJuliaCallHandle Call a named Julia function and return a handle to the result, which stays in Julia for other functions to use. An argument \";\" marks where named arguments start, as in JuliaCallNamedArgs.\nJuliaEval Evaluate a Julia expression and return the result to an Excel worksheet, or with ReturnHandle TRUE, a handle to the result.\nJuliaInclude Load a Julia source file into the Julia process, to make additional functions available via JuliaEval and JuliaCall.\nJuliaSetVar Set a global variable in the Julia process.\nJuliaHandleInfo Information about object handles: how many are live, or recent events.\nJuliaIsRunning Returns TRUE if an instance of Julia is running and \"listening\" to the current Excel session, or FALSE otherwise.\nJuliaExcelPID Returns the process id of the current Excel session. Only needed if more than one Excel instance is running - pass it to serve_xl to say which one to attach to.","title":"Worksheet functions"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#julialaunch","page":"Worksheet functions","text":"Launches a local Julia session which listens to the current Excel session and responds to calls to JuliaEval etc. Uses only its own arguments, not the ribbon's Launch Settings (see The Julia ribbon tab).\n\nJuliaLaunch([UseLinux], [MinimiseWindow], [CommandLineOptions], [Packages], [BashStatements], [TimeOut])\n\nArgument Description\nUseLinux TRUE to run Julia as a Linux process under Windows Subsystem for Linux; FALSE (the default) to run as a Windows process.\nMinimiseWindow If TRUE, then the Julia session window is minimised; if FALSE (the default) then the window is sized normally.\nCommandLineOptions Command line options for Julia, e.g. --project=C:\\MyProject or --banner=no. A --project folder must exist; --project=@temp starts Julia in a temporary environment, deleted when Julia exits (Julia 1.11 or later). If CommandLineOptions has no --threads option, --threads=auto,1 is added: Julia's usual threads, plus one interactive thread, which HTTP.jl recommends for servers. To choose the number of threads yourself, give your own --threads option, e.g. --threads=8,1 (the ,1 keeps the interactive thread). See Julia's command line options.\nPackages Packages to load, which must be installed in Julia's active environment (set by the --project command line option) or the default environment. Delimit multiple packages with commas. With --project=@temp, any that aren't installed are first installed into the temporary environment, preferring versions you already have, so they're already precompiled, and then loaded like the others.\nBashStatements Relevant only when UseLinux is TRUE. Bash statements executed prior to launching Julia, which can be used to set environment variables. Example: export JULIA_PKG_DEVDIR=/mnt/c/Projects. Delimit multiple statements with the line feed character.\nTimeOut The number of seconds to wait for Julia to fully start (including any package precompilation) before JuliaLaunch gives up waiting and returns an informational message rather than an error - Julia is not killed, and calling JuliaLaunch or JuliaEval again once it has finished starting will work normally. If Julia exits during start-up, e.g. because of an invalid command line option, JuliaLaunch reports an error straight away. TimeOut is optional and defaults to 30.\n\nExample. =JuliaLaunch(, TRUE, \"--threads=4,1\") launches Julia with its window minimised, four worker threads and one interactive thread; then =JuliaEval(\"Threads.nthreads()\") returns 4.","title":"JuliaLaunch"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#juliainclude","page":"Worksheet functions","text":"Load a Julia source file into the Julia process, to make additional functions available via JuliaEval and JuliaCall.\n\nJuliaInclude(FileName)\n\nArgument Description\nFileName The full name of the file to be included.\n\nThe ribbon's Include button does the same for the file in its File box, without needing a formula.\n\nExample. If C:\\MyCode\\collatz.jl contains\n\ncollatz(n) = n == 1 ? 0 : 1 + collatz(iseven(n) ? n / 2 : 3n + 1)\n\nthen after =JuliaInclude(\"C:\\MyCode\\collatz.jl\"), =JuliaCall(\"collatz\", 27) returns 111: starting from 27, the Collatz sequence takes 111 steps to reach 1.","title":"JuliaInclude"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#juliaeval","page":"Worksheet functions","text":"Evaluate a Julia expression and return the result to an Excel worksheet, or with ReturnHandle TRUE, a handle to the result.\n\nJuliaEval(JuliaExpression, [ReturnHandle])\n\nArgument Description\nJuliaExpression Any valid Julia code, as a string. Can also be a one-column range to evaluate multiple Julia statements.\nReturnHandle Optional, default FALSE. TRUE returns a handle to the result, which stays in Julia while a formula uses it. The code is evaluated again only when it changes. From VBA, use JuliaEvalHandle instead.\n\nExample. =JuliaEval(\"string(factorial(big(30)))\") returns 30! exactly, 265252859812191058636308480000000, using Julia's arbitrary-precision integers. Excel's =FACT(30) shows only 15 significant digits.","title":"JuliaEval"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#juliacall","page":"Worksheet functions","text":"Call a named Julia function, passing in data from the worksheet. Returns an error string for results that cannot be displayed on a worksheet (nested arrays, dictionaries etc.). JuliaCallVBA lifts those restrictions.\n\nJuliaCall(JuliaFunction, [Arg1], [Arg2], ...)\n\nArgument Description\nJuliaFunction The name of a Julia function visible from the Julia REPL.\nArgs... Zero or more arguments. Each may be a number, string, Boolean, empty cell, array or range. A range's values are passed as Excel stores them (its Value2), so cells formatted as dates arrive as serial numbers, not as Julia Dates.\n\nExample. With the coefficients of three linear equations in A1:C3 and their right-hand sides in D1:D3, =JuliaCall(\"\\\", A1:C3, D1:D3) solves them, returning the solution as a column. Julia's backslash operator solves the system directly, which is more accurate than Excel's =MMULT(MINVERSE(A1:C3), D1:D3). A trailing . on the function name broadcasts: =JuliaCall(\"bitstring.\", H1:H3) shows the 64 bits Julia stores for each number, which shows why 0.1 can't be stored exactly.","title":"JuliaCall"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#juliasetvar","page":"Worksheet functions","text":"Set a global variable in the Julia process.\n\nJuliaSetVar(VariableName, RefersTo)\n\nArgument Description\nVariableName The name of the variable to be set. Must follow Julia's rules for allowed variable names.\nRefersTo A range, number, string, Boolean, empty cell or array of such types. As for JuliaCall, cells formatted as dates arrive as serial numbers. When called from VBA, nested arrays are supported.\n\nExample. With prices in B1:B5, =JuliaSetVar(\"prices\", B1:B5) makes them a Julia variable, and then =JuliaEval(\"diff(log.(prices))\") returns their log returns as a column.","title":"JuliaSetVar"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#juliacallnamedargs","page":"Worksheet functions","text":"Call a named Julia function, passing positional and named arguments from the worksheet. The argument \";\" marks where the named arguments start, given as name-value pairs. A named argument whose value is \";\" must be the last pair.\n\nJuliaCallNamedArgs(JuliaFunction, [Arg1], ..., \";\", Name1, Value1, ...)\n\nArgument Description\nJuliaFunction The name of a Julia function visible from the Julia REPL.\nArgs... Zero or more positional arguments, then \";\", then pairs of name and value, e.g. \"a\",1,\"b\",2. Names must be valid Julia identifiers. Each argument may be a number, string, Boolean, empty cell, array or range.\n\nNamed arguments are what Julia's documentation and error messages call keyword arguments. The \";\" mirrors Julia's own call syntax, so for example =JuliaCallNamedArgs(\"sort\", A1:A10, \";\", \"rev\", TRUE) calls sort(A1:A10; rev=true). The \";\" is required, and must be followed by at least one name-value pair, so an even number of arguments. Otherwise JuliaCallNamedArgs returns an error saying so, rather than letting the call go ahead with misplaced arguments. To call a function without named arguments, use JuliaCall.\n\nJulia finds the \";\" by searching backwards from the end of the arguments, in steps of two (since name-value pairs follow it), and stops at the first \";\" it finds. So a \";\" among the positional arguments is never mistaken for the marker: =JuliaCallNamedArgs(\"split\", A1, \";\", \";\", \"keepempty\", FALSE) calls split(A1, \";\"; keepempty=false). The cost is that if a named argument's value is \";\", that pair must be given last, since a \";\" value in any earlier pair would be found first and taken as the marker.\n\nA trailing . on the function name broadcasts over the positional arguments, as for JuliaCall. As with f.(x; k=v) in Julia, the named arguments are passed unchanged to every call rather than broadcast over.","title":"JuliaCallNamedArgs"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#juliaisrunning","page":"Worksheet functions","text":"Returns TRUE if an instance of Julia is running and \"listening\" to the current Excel session, or FALSE otherwise.\n\nJuliaIsRunning()","title":"JuliaIsRunning"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#juliaexcelpid","page":"Worksheet functions","text":"Returns the process id of the current Excel session. You'll rarely need this directly: CallFromExcel.serve_xl() finds the running Excel process automatically when only one is open. It's only when more than one Excel instance is running that you need it, to tell CallFromExcel.serve_xl explicitly which one to attach to - see Attaching to a running Julia session.\n\nJuliaExcelPID()","title":"JuliaExcelPID"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#juliacallhandle","page":"Worksheet functions","text":"Call a named Julia function and return a handle to the result, which stays in Julia for other functions to use. An argument \";\" marks where named arguments start, as in JuliaCallNamedArgs.\n\nJuliaCallHandle(JuliaFunction, [Arg1], [Arg2], ..., [\";\", Name1, Value1, ...])\n\nArgument Description\nJuliaFunction The name of a Julia function visible from the Julia REPL. A trailing . broadcasts, as for JuliaCall.\nArgs... Zero or more positional arguments, optionally followed by \";\" and pairs of name and value for named arguments, as for JuliaCallNamedArgs. Handles among them are replaced by their objects.\n\nExample. With a list of values in G1:G20, =JuliaCallHandle(\"Set\", G1:G20) in H1 builds a Julia Set once, and then =JuliaCall(\"in\", D1, H1) tests whether D1's value is in the list, without searching it each time.\n\nUnlike JuliaCallNamedArgs, any \";\" argument starts the named arguments, so \";\" can't be passed as a positional argument.\n\nUnlike JuliaCall, an error value as an argument, such as #N/A, is returned rather than passed to Julia: see errors pass through.\n\nFrom VBA, jl.JuliaCallHandle takes at most 30 arguments after the function name, and, as every function called from VBA, raises an error if the call fails. The handle lasts until JuliaHandleRelease or JuliaHandleReleaseAll frees it.","title":"JuliaCallHandle"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#juliahandleinfo","page":"Worksheet functions","text":"Information about object handles: how many are live, or recent events.\n\nJuliaHandleInfo(Type)\n\nArgument Description\nType \"count\" for the number of live handles created by worksheet formulas, and of objects Julia holds (which includes those created from VBA). \"events\" for a column of recent create and release events, newest last.","title":"JuliaHandleInfo"},{"category":"section","location":"marshalling/#Marshalling","page":"Marshalling","text":"Two questions arose during implementation. First, what Julia type should data from a worksheet or VBA arrive as? Mostly that's easy to decide, but should a single-column or single-row range, or a one-dimensional VBA array, have one dimension or two in Julia? Second, in the other direction, how should a Julia vector come back to Excel?\n\nThe design had three objectives:\n\nRound-tripping: =JuliaCall(\"identity\",x) should return an identical copy of x, whatever its shape.\nNatural matrix arithmetic: =JuliaCall(\"*\",Range1,Range2) should give the same result as =MMULT(Range1,Range2), since * in Julia is matrix multiplication.\nJulia's dot syntax for broadcasting should work from JuliaCall.\n\nThis scheme achieves them:\n\nScalars marshal back and forth as scalars.\nRanges or arrays with more than one row and more than one column marshal back and forth as two-dimensional arrays.\nA single-column range arrives in Julia as a vector, and a Julia vector returns to the worksheet as a single column.\nA single-row range arrives in Julia as a two-dimensional array with one row.\n\nFrom VBA, one-dimensional arrays arrive in Julia as vectors, and JuliaCallVBA and JuliaEvalVBA return Julia vectors as one-dimensional arrays, so VBA variables round-trip too.\n\ndetails: Round-tripping of vectors and matrices\n(Image: Round-tripping vectors and matrices between an Excel worksheet and Julia)\n\ndetails: Matrix arithmetic\n(Image: Matrix arithmetic in Julia on Excel ranges, results spilling as dynamic arrays)\n\ndetails: Function broadcasting\n(Image: Broadcasting a Julia function over Excel ranges with JuliaCall)","title":"Marshalling"},{"category":"section","location":"marshalling/#numbers-from-a-worksheet-arrive-as-float64","page":"Marshalling","text":"Excel stores every number on a worksheet as a double-precision float, so numbers passed from a worksheet to JuliaCall, JuliaCallNamedArgs or JuliaSetVar always arrive in Julia as Float64, even whole numbers such as 2. This includes the results of other functions in the same formula, since Excel converts a function's result to a worksheet value before passing it on. So =JuliaCall(\"typeof\", JuliaEval(\"2\")) returns Float64, even though JuliaEval(\"2\") returns an Int64 to VBA (but see the third workaround below).\n\nThis matters when calling a Julia function that requires an integer, whether as a positional argument (e.g. rand(3)) or a named one (e.g. round(x; digits=2), split(s, \",\"; limit=2)). Passing Float64 gives a MethodError or TypeError. There are three workarounds:\n\nWrite the whole call in JuliaEval, e.g. =JuliaEval(\"rand(3)\"), if the integer is a constant.\nWrite a small Julia function that converts, e.g. myrand(n) = rand(Int(n)), loaded with JuliaInclude, and call that instead.\nPass the integer as a handle. JuliaEval(\"3\", TRUE) evaluates 3 in Julia and returns a handle to the resulting Int64, which Julia receives as the integer itself, not as a worksheet number. So =JuliaCall(\"rand\", JuliaEval(\"3\", TRUE)) calls rand(3), and =JuliaCallNamedArgs(\"round\", A1, \";\", \"digits\", JuliaEval(\"2\", TRUE)) calls round(A1; digits=2). Unlike the first workaround, the integer can come from a cell: JuliaEval(B1, TRUE) evaluates B1's number as Julia code.\n\nFrom VBA, numbers keep their VBA type: Integer arrives as Int16, Long as Int32, LongLong as Int64, Single as Float32 and Double as Float64. So pass a LongLong (e.g. 2^ or CLngLng(n)) where Julia needs an Int64. Note that a literal such as 2 in VBA code is an Integer, which arrives as Int16 - some Julia functions, such as split, require an Int64 and won't accept that either.\n\nJulia's integer arithmetic wraps around on overflow rather than raising an error, and the narrower the type, the sooner that happens. So from VBA, jl.JuliaCall(\"*\", 200, 200) returns -25536, since both arguments arrive as Int16, where VBA itself would raise an Overflow error. Pass LongLong or Double values when results may be large.","title":"Numbers from a worksheet arrive as Float64"},{"category":"section","location":"marshalling/#Complex-numbers-return-as-text","page":"Marshalling","text":"Excel has no complex number type. Its functions such as IMREAL, IMAGINARY and IMSUM work on text such as \"3+4i\" instead. So complex numbers in Julia return to Excel as text in exactly the format of Excel's own COMPLEX function, e.g. \"3+4i\", \"-i\" or \"1.5+i\", with 15 significant digits. As with COMPLEX, a number with a zero imaginary part returns as text without one, e.g. \"3\". Text in this form passed from Excel to Julia arrives as a String, not as a complex number.","title":"Complex numbers return as text"},{"category":"section","location":"marshalling/#Dates-use-the-1900-date-system","page":"Marshalling","text":"Dates and times returned from Julia to a worksheet become Excel date serial numbers in the 1900 date system, Excel's default on Windows. In a workbook set to the 1904 date system (File > Options > Advanced > \"Use 1904 date system\"), they display 4 years and 1 day late. Dates passed from a worksheet to Julia arrive as plain numbers, as Excel stores them, whichever date system the workbook uses. Calls from VBA are unaffected, since VBA dates aren't serial numbers.","title":"Dates use the 1900 date system"},{"category":"section","location":"own_functions/#Your-own-worksheet-functions","page":"Your own worksheet functions","text":"Instead of =JuliaCall(\"birthday\", A1), a Julia function can be a worksheet function of its own, =jl.birthday(A1), with its arguments and description shown by Excel's Insert Function (fx) dialog and in the tooltips as you type. Mark it with @excel:\n\n\"\"\"\n    birthday(people, days=365)\n\nThe probability that, in a group of people, at least two share a birthday.\n\n# Arguments\n- `people`: the number of people in the group.\n- `days`: the number of days in the year.\n\"\"\"\n@excel broadcast=true function birthday(people, days=365)\n    1 - prod((days - k) / days for k in 0:people-1)\nend\n\nInclude the file - with JuliaInclude, or the ribbon's Include button - and =jl.birthday(23) works in any cell of any open workbook, returning 0.507: in a group of just 23 people, it's more likely than not that two share a birthday. broadcast=true makes it work on ranges too: with the numbers 1 to 60 in A1:A60, =jl.birthday(A1:A60) returns a column of 60 probabilities, ready to chart. Typing =jl. lists all such functions. A Julia function that's already defined, including one from a package, can be made available without changing its definition with expose_to_excel, for example expose_to_excel(\"hypot\"), or expose_to_excel(LinearAlgebra.det) for =jl.det(A1:C3).\n\n@excel and expose_to_excel take the same options:\n\nOption Description\nname The Excel name. Default \"jl.\" followed by the Julia name. The prefix keeps your functions apart from Excel's own (when names clash, Excel's built-in function wins) and from cell references: abc123 is a cell, jl.abc123 isn't. A name without it, such as name=\"BIRTHDAY\", is allowed. A name Excel can't use is refused with an error saying why: one with characters Excel doesn't allow (push!), one Excel would read as a cell reference, one beginning with Julia (reserved for the add-in's functions), or one differing only in case from a name already in use, since Excel ignores case.\ndescription, argdescriptions Descriptions of the function and of each argument, for the Insert Function dialog. By default they come from the docstring: the first paragraph after the signature, and the # Arguments list. Each is cut to 253 characters, Excel's limit.\nbroadcast Which arguments, given a range, are broadcast over - see below. Default false, none.\nhandle true to return a handle to the result, which stays in Julia, rather than the result itself - see below. Default false.\nvolatile true to recalculate at every calculation, as Excel's NOW and RAND do - see below. Default false.\n\nPoints to note:\n\nOptional arguments. Arguments with default values (days=365) are optional: leave them out at the end of a call, as in =jl.birthday(23), and Julia uses the defaults; give them to override the defaults, as in =jl.birthday(23, 366). Leaving out a required argument (=jl.birthday()), or one before an argument that is given (=jl.birthday(, 366)), is an error naming it. Keyword arguments aren't available as worksheet arguments; use JuliaCallNamedArgs for those.\nWhich method. @excel follows the method it's attached to. expose_to_excel follows the method with the most arguments, not counting methods with a variable number of arguments (f(x...)).\nRanges are passed whole. By default a range arrives in Julia as a matrix (a single cell as a single value), as with JuliaCall. An argument declared ::AbstractVector receives a vector, from a single cell, a row or a column.\nBroadcasting. For a function written for single values, broadcast makes it work on ranges: a range given to a broadcasting argument is broadcast over, as Julia's f.(x, y) does, in one call from Excel, and the result spills. broadcast=true broadcasts every argument, except those declared as arrays (w::AbstractVector), which are passed whole; broadcast=(:x, :y), or broadcast=:x for one, names the arguments to broadcast. For example, birthday above is marked broadcast=true, so =jl.birthday(A1:A60) returns a column of 60 probabilities, and with 365 and 366 in B1:C1, =jl.birthday(A1:A60, B1:C1) - a column against a row - a 60 by 2 table. Without broadcast=true, birthday would receive A1:A60 as a matrix, and fail. Ranges broadcast together must have the same number of rows, or one of them a single row, and likewise for columns; otherwise the error names the two arguments (where Excel would pad with #N/A). A broadcast function that returns an array for each element gives an error, as the result can't be shown in one range.\nReturning a handle. For a function whose result belongs in Julia rather than on the sheet - a fitted model, a large matrix, a dictionary - use handle=true, and the worksheet function returns a handle to it, as JuliaCallHandle does. For example, with @excel handle=true squarearray(n) = reshape(1:Int(n)^2, Int(n), Int(n)), =jl.squarearray(1000) in A1 returns a handle such as jl:ReshapedArray#1@k3x9, and =JuliaCall(\"sum\", A1) sums the million elements without them ever reaching the sheet. Handles' rules apply: the same arguments give the same object, built once, and it's freed when no formula uses it. With broadcasting, the handle is to the whole broadcast result. Excel's description of the function ends \"Returns a handle.\", and =JuliaCall(\"identity\", jl.squarearray(3)) gets the result itself.\nVolatile functions. Excel recalculates a cell when its inputs change, so a function that reads something Excel can't see - a Julia global set by JuliaSetVar or JuliaEval, a file, a database, the clock, random numbers - can show an out-of-date result, and a function with no arguments is recalculated only by Ctrl+Alt+F9. With volatile=true, as in @excel volatile=true latest_price(ticker) = ..., Excel recalculates it at every calculation, as it does NOW and RAND. Use it sparingly: every calculation (every edit, in automatic calculation) calls Julia for each cell using the function, and recalculates the cells that depend on them. volatile=true can't be combined with handle=true, as a handle keeps its object while the arguments are unchanged.\nWhen functions are registered. After JuliaLaunch, JuliaInclude and JuliaEval, and the ribbon's Launch Julia and Include buttons, the add-in registers any functions that have been exposed since it last did, once Excel is next idle. Functions exposed from the Julia REPL, or from VBA's jl.JuliaEval or jl.JuliaInclude, are registered by the ribbon's Register Functions button, or from VBA with Application.Run \"JuliaRegisterFunctions\", which registers them straight away. Registered functions stay registered until Excel closes: a function no longer exposed in a new Julia session gives an error saying so.\nFormulas typed too soon show #NAME?. A formula that uses a function before it is registered shows #NAME?, and goes on doing so after registration, until it is re-entered (select it, press F2, then Enter). This doesn't affect saved workbooks: reopened before Julia is running, their cells keep their saved values, and recalculate correctly once the functions are registered (in manual calculation mode, at the next recalculation). VBA code that writes such formulas should run Application.Run \"JuliaRegisterFunctions\" first.\nChanging a function. Change its code and re-include the file: the worksheet function calls whatever the Julia function now does, with no need to register again. (Cells using it aren't recalculated by the change; press Ctrl+Alt+F9.) Changing its arguments needs it to be exposed again, which re-including a file with @excel does.\nFrom VBA. Call the Julia function itself, as in jl.JuliaCallVBA(\"birthday\", 23) (see Calling from VBA), rather than the worksheet function through Application.Run(\"jl.birthday\", 23). That avoids a detour through Excel, allows results a worksheet can't hold (such as a dictionary), and makes the code independent of whether the function has been registered. Use the Julia name, not the Excel name, and leave out optional arguments as usual. The @excel options don't apply. To broadcast, add a . to the name, as in jl.JuliaCallVBA(\"birthday.\", sizes), which broadcasts over every argument. For a function with handle=true, jl.JuliaCallHandle(\"squarearray\", 1000) returns a handle that lasts until jl.JuliaHandleRelease frees it, whereas through Application.Run the handle is freed straight away.\n\nSee also @excel and expose_to_excel in the Julia API.","title":"Your own worksheet functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Julia-API","page":"Julia API","text":"The Julia functions of the CallFromExcel package that you might call yourself. The worksheet functions, which are part of the Excel add-in, are documented in Worksheet functions and VBA functions.","title":"Julia API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Installation","page":"Julia API","text":"","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Your-own-worksheet-functions","page":"Julia API","text":"","title":"Your own worksheet functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Object-handles","page":"Julia API","text":"","title":"Object handles"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Attaching-to-a-running-Julia-session","page":"Julia API","text":"","title":"Attaching to a running Julia session"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Debugging","page":"Julia API","text":"The variables last_question, last_answer, args_from_xl and kwargs_from_xl are described in Debugging.","title":"Debugging"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Types","page":"Julia API","text":"","title":"Types"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CallFromExcel.installme","page":"Julia API","text":"installme()\n\nInstall CallFromExcel's Excel add-in for the current user, from this version of the package, and uninstall JuliaExcel's add-in if present. Needs no admin rights. Close Excel first: the installer stops with an error if Excel is running.\n\nRun it after installing CallFromExcel, and again after each update: the add-in and the Julia package must be the same version. If they aren't, Excel says so on launching Julia.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CallFromExcel.installme"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#CallFromExcel.@excel","page":"Julia API","text":"@excel [name=\"...\"] [description=\"...\"] [argdescriptions=[...]] [broadcast=...] [handle=...] [volatile=...] function f(...) ... end\n\nDefine a function and make it callable from Excel, as jl.f by default, following the method defined here (rather than the one with the most arguments). Options are as for expose_to_excel, e.g. @excel broadcast=true f(x, y) = .... A docstring goes before @excel, as before the function.\n\n\"\"\"\n    birthday(people, days=365)\n\nThe probability that, in a group of people, at least two share a birthday.\n\"\"\"\n@excel broadcast=true function birthday(people, days=365)\n    1 - prod((days - k) / days for k in 0:people-1)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CallFromExcel.@excel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CallFromExcel.expose_to_excel","page":"Julia API","text":"expose_to_excel(fn_name; name=nothing, description=\"\", argdescriptions=String[], broadcast=false, handle=false, volatile=false)\nexpose_to_excel(f::Function; ...)\n\nMake a Julia function, named by fn_name (evaluated in Main) or given itself, callable from Excel as a worksheet function, by default jl.<its name>. Returns the Excel name. The add-in registers exposed functions after JuliaLaunch, JuliaInclude and JuliaEval, and when the ribbon's Register Functions button is clicked (for functions exposed from the REPL). @excel does the same for the method it's attached to.\n\nThe Excel function's arguments are those of the method with the most positional arguments (not counting varargs methods). Arguments with default values (f(a, b=2)) are optional in Excel: left out at the end of a call, they take their defaults.\n\nDescriptions, shown by Excel's Function Wizard and IntelliSense, come from the function's docstring unless given: the first paragraph after the signature describes the function, and an # Arguments list (- name description) its arguments. argdescriptions replaces the docstring's, position by position, where not empty.\n\nBroadcasting\n\nExcel passes a range as a matrix, and by default the function receives it whole. A range given to a broadcasting argument is instead broadcast over, as f.(...) would, so a function written for single values works on ranges, with one call from Excel. Other arguments are passed whole to every call. Which arguments broadcast:\n\nbroadcast=false (the default): none.\nbroadcast=true: all, except any declared as arrays (c::AbstractVector), which are passed whole.\nbroadcast=(:a, :b), or broadcast=:a for one: those named.\n\nAn argument declared ::AbstractVector gets a vector from a single cell, row or column. Ranges broadcast together must have the same number of rows, or one of them a single row (and likewise columns); a column against a row gives a table, as in Excel.\n\nHandles\n\nWith handle=true, the Excel function returns a handle to the result, which stays in Julia, as JuliaCallHandle does: for a fitted model, a large matrix or anything else that belongs in Julia rather than on the sheet. The same arguments give the same object, built once. With broadcasting, the handle is to the whole broadcast result. The description shown in Excel ends \"Returns a handle.\"\n\nVolatile functions\n\nWith volatile=true, Excel recalculates the function at every calculation, as it does NOW and RAND, rather than only when its arguments change: for a function that reads something Excel can't see, such as a Julia global set by JuliaSetVar or JuliaEval, a file, a database, the clock or a random number generator. Use it sparingly: every calculation calls Julia for every cell using the function, and recalculates the cells that depend on them. volatile=true can't be combined with handle=true, since a handle keeps its object while the arguments are unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CallFromExcel.expose_to_excel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CallFromExcel.handle_label","page":"Julia API","text":"handle_label(x)\n\nThe label in a handle for x, jl:<label>#<id>@<session>, for people reading the sheet - looking up the object ignores it. By default the name of x's type, without its module or parameters: Array for a Matrix{Float64}, Dict for a Dict{String,Int}.\n\nAdd methods for your own types to choose a more informative label, for example:\n\nCallFromExcel.handle_label(x::YieldCurve) = \"Curve_$(x.currency)\"\n\nso that a handle to a US dollar curve reads jl:Curve_USD#3@k3x9. The label is worked out once, when the handle is created. Any #, @, whitespace or control character in it is replaced by _, since # and @ mark where the id and session start.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CallFromExcel.handle_label"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CallFromExcel.serve_xl","page":"Julia API","text":"serve_xl(show_results::Bool=true, pid::Integer=0)\n\nAttaches this Julia session to an Excel process, so it responds to JuliaCall/JuliaEval requests from that Excel session exactly as if it had been launched by the JuliaLaunch() worksheet function.\n\nshow_results is passed to display_results - it defaults to true here, since attaching an interactive session like this is exactly when seeing each call and its result echoed to the REPL is most useful.\n\npid identifies the Excel process to attach to. Leave it at its default of 0 to attach to the single running Excel process automatically - if none is found, or more than one is, throws an error explaining how to call serve_xl with pid given explicitly. Get pid from Excel's JuliaExcelPID() worksheet function.\n\nGiven explicitly, pid is checked against the running Excel processes (Windows only - this check is skipped, not enforced, when Julia itself is running under WSL) and an error thrown if it doesn't match one, rather than silently starting a server nothing will ever connect to.\n\nAlso throws if Excel already has another Julia session listening for it (e.g. one launched via JuliaLaunch, or attached earlier with serve_xl itself) - starting a second one wouldn't do anything useful, since Excel would keep talking to whichever session it's already connected to. Stop that session first (e.g. call stop_server() there, or close it) before attaching this one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CallFromExcel.serve_xl"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CallFromExcel.stop_server","page":"Julia API","text":"stop_server()\n\nStops this Julia session's HTTP server, if one is running, so it no longer responds to JuliaCall/JuliaEval requests from Excel. No-op if no server is currently running.\n\nAlso clears the port from this session's window title (via settitle) - otherwise the title would keep claiming a port this session no longer serves, which could wrongly look like a match for whichever port a different session (e.g. one started with serve_xl) happens to pick up next.\n\nLeaves the port file on disk untouched, so Excel will still try the now-dead port on its next request - and get a clean \"no connection\" error rather than reaching this session again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CallFromExcel.stop_server"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CallFromExcel.server_status","page":"Julia API","text":"server_status()\n\nReturns a NamedTuple (running, pid, port, display_results) describing this Julia session's HTTP server: running is true if a server is currently listening; pid is the Excel process id it's set up to serve (0 if setxlpid has never been called), regardless of whether the server is currently running; port is the port it's listening on, or 0 if not running; display_results is the current setting of display_results().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CallFromExcel.server_status"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CallFromExcel.display_results","page":"Julia API","text":"display_results(switch::Bool)\n\nSwitch on or off display in the REPL of both the incoming expression/function call from Excel and the value returned to Excel, for calls via JuliaCall and JuliaEval.\n\n\n\n\n\ndisplay_results()\n\nReturns whether display in the REPL of results of calls from Excel is currently switched on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CallFromExcel.display_results"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CallFromExcel.answer_again","page":"Julia API","text":"answer_again()\n\nEvaluates last_question again, and you can wrap it with @enter or your own debugging tools. Useful for interactively debugging a JuliaCall/JuliaEval that failed (or just misbehaved) when called from Excel, without needing to repeat the call from there.\n\nUses Meta.parseall rather than Meta.parse since last_question set by srv_eval_inner can be a multi-statement, newline-separated string (see its docstring) - Meta.parseall also handles the single-statement string that srv_call_inner sets last_question to, so this is correct either way.\n\nSee also last_question, last_answer, args_from_xl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CallFromExcel.answer_again"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CallFromExcel.ExcelError","page":"Julia API","text":"ExcelError(code::Int)\n\nRepresents an Excel error value (e.g. #DIV/0!, #N/A) received from Excel. Decoded from the wire format's ! type indicator (see decode_from_xl in decode.jl) and re-encoded the same way by encode_for_xl (encode.jl), so an Excel error round-trips through Julia unchanged - e.g. JuliaCall(\"identity\", SomeErrorCell) returns the same error.\n\nA Julia function that isn't written to expect an Excel error, and receives one where it expects an ordinary value, fails immediately with a MethodError. Functions that want to handle Excel errors explicitly can dispatch on this type, e.g. myfunc(::ExcelError) = missing to swallow them, or myfunc(e::ExcelError) = e to propagate them onward unchanged.\n\nThe numeric code is stored as-is, with no validation against the current list of known Excel error codes - Excel could add new ones in future versions, and any code decodes/re-encodes identically regardless of whether it's recognised (see EXCEL_ERROR_NAMES below, used only for display).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CallFromExcel.ExcelError"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Performance-and-alternatives","page":"Performance and alternatives","text":"","title":"Performance and alternatives"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Performance","page":"Performance and alternatives","text":"Calls are fast: a simple call takes under a millisecond, and =JuliaCall(\"sort\", A1:A1000000) sorts a million numbers in about 0.4 seconds, including sending them to Julia and back. That's faster than Excel's own =SORT(A1:A1000000), which takes about 0.6 seconds, and about a third of the time taken by CallFromExcel's predecessor, JuliaExcel. See the changelog for more timings.","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Alternatives","page":"Performance and alternatives","text":"Apart from CallFromExcel's own predecessor, JuliaExcel, there is one other method of calling Julia from Excel of which I am aware: JuliaInXL.jl.\n\nJuliaComputing (now JuliaHub) made JuliaInXL open source in October 2021; it previously required a licence for commercial use. When last checked (November 2026), JuliaInXL did not support dynamic array formulas or calling Julia from VBA. Filling those gaps was why I wrote JuliaExcel, this project's predecessor, in 2021.\n\nEarly versions of JuliaExcel were slower than JuliaInXL, both in latency and in moving data between Excel and Julia. That has since reversed: in tests (see workbooks/CallFromExcel-Compare.xlsm in the repository), CallFromExcel was between 1.6 and 16 times faster. For example, =jlcall(\"sort\", A1:A1000000) takes about 4.5 seconds, and =JuliaCall(\"sort\", A1:A1000000) about 0.4 seconds.","title":"Alternatives"},{"category":"section","location":"debugging/#Debugging","page":"Debugging","text":"Every call to JuliaCall/JuliaCallVBA/JuliaEval/JuliaEvalVBA leaves a trace behind in Julia, so a problem can be debugged at the REPL without needing to repeat the call from Excel:\n\nCallFromExcel.last_question is the expression that was evaluated - either the exact string passed to JuliaEval/JuliaEvalVBA, or, for JuliaCall/JuliaCallVBA, a reconstructed call such as myfunction(CallFromExcel.args_from_xl...).\nCallFromExcel.last_answer is the value that was returned to Excel.\nCallFromExcel.args_from_xl holds the arguments most recently passed from Excel to JuliaCall/JuliaCallVBA, or the positional arguments for JuliaCallNamedArgs/JuliaCallNamedArgsVBA. For those, CallFromExcel.kwargs_from_xl holds the named arguments as a NamedTuple, and last_question is a call such as myfunction(args_from_xl...; kwargs_from_xl...).\nCallFromExcel.answer_again() evaluates last_question again, and you can wrap it with @enter or your own debugging tools.\n\nAll of these only reflect the most recent call, so debug before making another one. Call CallFromExcel.display_results(true), or tick Display Results on the ribbon, to also have each call and its result echoed to the Julia console as it happens.\n\nTo step through your code with a debugger, attach a Julia session you already have open, such as one in VS Code - see Attaching to a running Julia session.","title":"Debugging"},{"category":"section","location":"vba_functions/#VBA-functions","page":"VBA functions","text":"These functions can only be called from VBA, through the object described in Calling from VBA, as for example jl.JuliaCallVBA(...). VBA can also call the worksheet functions, all except JuliaHandleInfo.\n\nName Description\nJuliaEvalVBA Evaluate a Julia expression from VBA. Differs from JuliaCall in handling of 1-d arrays and strings longer than 32,767 characters. May return data of types that cannot be displayed on a worksheet, such as a dictionary or an array of arrays.\nJuliaCallVBA Call a named Julia function from VBA. Differs from JuliaCall in handling of 1-d arrays and strings longer than 32,767 characters. May return data of types that cannot be displayed on a worksheet, such as a dictionary, an array of arrays, or arrays of dimension up to 9.\nJuliaCallNamedArgsVBA Call a named Julia function from VBA, passing positional and named arguments, with \";\" marking where the named arguments start. Differs from JuliaCallNamedArgs in the same ways that JuliaCallVBA differs from JuliaCall.\nJuliaEvalHandle Evaluate a Julia expression from VBA and return a handle to the result. The VBA counterpart of JuliaEval(JuliaExpression, TRUE).\nJuliaHandleRelease Free the Julia object of a handle created from VBA.\nJuliaHandleReleaseAll Free the Julia objects of all handles created from VBA.","title":"VBA functions"},{"category":"section","location":"vba_functions/#juliaevalvba","page":"VBA functions","text":"Evaluate a Julia expression from VBA. Differs from JuliaCall in handling of 1-d arrays and strings longer than 32,767 characters. May return data of types that cannot be displayed on a worksheet, such as a dictionary or an array of arrays.\n\njl.JuliaEvalVBA(JuliaExpression)\n\nArgument Description\nJuliaExpression Any valid Julia code, as a string. Can also be a one-column range to evaluate multiple Julia statements.\n\nExample. Set d = jl.JuliaEvalVBA(\"Dict(w => length(w) for w in split(\"\"the quick brown fox\"\"))\") returns a Scripting.Dictionary mapping each word to its length, so d(\"quick\") is 5. A dictionary can't be returned to a worksheet.","title":"JuliaEvalVBA"},{"category":"section","location":"vba_functions/#juliacallvba","page":"VBA functions","text":"Call a named Julia function from VBA. Differs from JuliaCall in handling of 1-d arrays and strings longer than 32,767 characters. May return data of types that cannot be displayed on a worksheet, such as a dictionary, an array of arrays, or arrays of dimension up to 9.\n\njl.JuliaCallVBA(JuliaFunction, [Arg1], [Arg2], ...)\n\nArgument Description\nJuliaFunction The name of a Julia function visible from the Julia REPL.\nArgs... Zero or more arguments. Each may be a number, string, Boolean, empty cell, array or range. A range's values are passed as Excel stores them (its Value2), so cells formatted as dates arrive as serial numbers, not as Julia Dates.\n\nExample. words = jl.JuliaCallVBA(\"split\", \"the quick brown fox\") returns a one-dimensional VBA array, words(1) to words(4).","title":"JuliaCallVBA"},{"category":"section","location":"vba_functions/#juliacallnamedargsvba","page":"VBA functions","text":"Call a named Julia function from VBA, passing positional and named arguments, with \";\" marking where the named arguments start. Differs from JuliaCallNamedArgs in the same ways that JuliaCallVBA differs from JuliaCall.\n\njl.JuliaCallNamedArgsVBA(JuliaFunction, [Arg1], ..., \";\", Name1, Value1, ...)\n\nArgument Description\nJuliaFunction The name of a Julia function visible from the Julia REPL.\nArgs... Zero or more positional arguments, then \";\", then pairs of name and value, e.g. \"a\",1,\"b\",2. Names must be valid Julia identifiers. Each argument may be a number, string, Boolean, empty cell, array or range.\n\nExample. parts = jl.JuliaCallNamedArgsVBA(\"split\", \"a,b,,c\", \",\", \";\", \"keepempty\", False) returns a, b and c, without the empty string between the two commas.","title":"JuliaCallNamedArgsVBA"},{"category":"section","location":"vba_functions/#juliaevalhandle","page":"VBA functions","text":"Evaluate a Julia expression from VBA and return a handle to the result. The VBA counterpart of JuliaEval(JuliaExpression, TRUE). Raises an error if the evaluation fails. The handle lasts until JuliaHandleRelease or JuliaHandleReleaseAll frees it.\n\njl.JuliaEvalHandle(JuliaExpression)\n\nArgument Description\nJuliaExpression Any valid Julia code, as a string. Can also be a one-column range to evaluate multiple Julia statements.\n\nExample.\n\nodds = jl.JuliaEvalHandle(\"Set(1:2:999)\")\nDebug.Print jl.JuliaCall(\"in\", 7, odds)   ' True\njl.JuliaHandleRelease odds","title":"JuliaEvalHandle"},{"category":"section","location":"vba_functions/#juliahandlerelease","page":"VBA functions","text":"Free the Julia object of a handle created from VBA. Returns TRUE if it freed one, and FALSE otherwise - when the handle was already freed, was created by a worksheet formula (which frees its own), or isn't a handle at all, such as an empty string. So it is always safe to call, for example from a class's Class_Terminate event.\n\njl.JuliaHandleRelease(Handle)\n\nArgument Description\nHandle A handle returned by jl.JuliaCallHandle or jl.JuliaEvalHandle.","title":"JuliaHandleRelease"},{"category":"section","location":"vba_functions/#juliahandlereleaseall","page":"VBA functions","text":"Free the Julia objects of all handles created from VBA, and return how many. Handles created by worksheet formulas are unaffected.\n\njl.JuliaHandleReleaseAll()","title":"JuliaHandleReleaseAll"},{"category":"section","location":"vba/#Calling-from-VBA","page":"Calling from VBA","text":"The VBA code below makes a call to JuliaLaunch and JuliaEvalVBA and then pastes the result to range A1:J10 in a new worksheet. VBA reaches CallFromExcel's functions through an object created with CreateObject(\"CallFromExcel.ComServer\"), which needs no reference. Alternatively, add a reference to CallFromExcel (VBA editor, Tools menu -> References) and declare Dim jl As New CallFromExcel.ComServer: the VBA editor then shows each function's arguments as you type. But a workbook with that reference won't compile on a PC without CallFromExcel installed.\n\nSub Demo()\n\n    Dim jl As Object, ResultFromJulia As Variant, PasteHere As Range\n\n    Set jl = CreateObject(\"CallFromExcel.ComServer\")\n    jl.JuliaLaunch\n\n    ResultFromJulia = jl.JuliaEvalVBA(\"(1:10).^(1:10)'\")\n\n    Set PasteHere = Application.Workbooks.Add.Worksheets(1).Range(\"A1:J10\")\n\n    PasteHere.Value = ResultFromJulia\n\nEnd Sub\n\nThe functions ending in VBA - JuliaEvalVBA, JuliaCallVBA and JuliaCallNamedArgsVBA - and JuliaEvalHandle, JuliaHandleRelease and JuliaHandleReleaseAll can only be called from VBA. From VBA, the JuliaCall family takes at most 30 arguments after the function name.","title":"Calling from VBA"},{"category":"section","location":"vba/#Errors","page":"Calling from VBA","text":"A call from VBA that fails raises an error, for On Error to handle, rather than returning a value. (On a worksheet, where a formula can't raise an error, the same failure shows as a string such as #MethodError: ... Julia REPL has more details and stacktrace!.) Err.Source is \"CallFromExcel\", Err.Description the message, and Err.Number one of:\n\nErr.Number Meaning\nvbObjectError + 1001 The Julia code threw an exception. Err.Description starts with its type, such as MethodError:, and the Julia REPL shows the full error and stack trace.\nvbObjectError + 1002 Julia isn't running: not yet launched, exited, or still starting.\nvbObjectError + 1003 Any other failure: a result that can't be returned, such as a dictionary from JuliaCall rather than JuliaCallVBA; an argument that can't be sent to Julia; or JuliaLaunch failing to start Julia.\n\nFor example:\n\nSub FitModel()\n    Dim jl As Object, Model As Variant\n    Set jl = CreateObject(\"CallFromExcel.ComServer\")\n\n    On Error GoTo Failed\n    Model = jl.JuliaCallVBA(\"fit_model\", Range(\"A1:A100\").Value, Range(\"B1:B100\").Value)\n    ' ...use Model...\n    Exit Sub\n\nFailed:\n    If Err.Number = vbObjectError + 1002 Then\n        MsgBox \"Please launch Julia first.\"\n    Else\n        MsgBox \"fit_model failed: \" & Err.Description\n    End If\nEnd Sub\n\nExcel error values are data, not failures: a result that is, or contains, #N/A or another error value is returned as such (IsError is True for it), and a string that merely looks like an error, such as \"#N/A\" or \"#Done!\", is returned as a string.\n\nUntil version 0.2.0, these functions returned failures as strings starting with #, which VBA code had to check for after each call.","title":"Errors"},{"category":"section","location":"attaching/#Attaching-to-a-running-Julia-session","page":"Attaching to a running Julia session","text":"Normally JuliaLaunch starts its own dedicated Julia process. If you'd rather use a Julia session you already have open - for example one running inside VS Code, where you can set breakpoints and step through code - you can attach that session to Excel instead:\n\nusing CallFromExcel\nserve_xl()\n\nWith no argument, serve_xl finds the single running Excel process automatically and attaches to it, also switching display_results on - since seeing each call arrive is exactly what you want when attached like this for interactive debugging. Pass false to leave display_results off: serve_xl(false).\n\nIf more than one Excel process is running, serve_xl won't guess - it throws, telling you to pass the process id explicitly instead: serve_xl(true, pid), getting pid from Excel's JuliaExcelPID() worksheet function.\n\nBack in Excel, JuliaCall/JuliaEval/etc. now talk to the attached session directly - there's no need to call JuliaLaunch at all, though if you do (e.g. from existing VBA code), it will detect the attached session and use it rather than starting a new one.\n\nIf Excel is already being served by another Julia session (e.g. one launched via JuliaLaunch, or attached earlier with serve_xl itself), serve_xl throws rather than starting a second, useless listener - stop the other session first (e.g. call stop_server() there, or close it).\n\nOther functions for managing a session started this way:\n\nCallFromExcel.stop_server() stops the session listening to Excel, without closing Julia itself.\nCallFromExcel.server_status() reports whether a server is currently running, which Excel process id it's serving, on which port, and the current display_results setting.\n\nOn the ribbon, Display Results works with an attached session, but Show Julia can't find its window, and Close Julia closes it - Julia and all - rather than just detaching it. To detach, call stop_server() in the session instead.\n\nWhen the Excel being served closes, the session stops serving it by itself, whether it was attached with serve_xl or started by JuliaLaunch. Julia stays open, so nothing in it is lost. (Not under WSL, where Julia can't watch a Windows process.)","title":"Attaching to a running Julia session"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Installation","page":"Installation","text":"Installation does not require admin rights on the PC.\n\nBoth Julia and Microsoft Office must be installed on your PC.\nClose Excel - installation fails if Excel is running.\nLaunch Julia, and copy-paste the following commands into the REPL. To paste into the REPL, right-click.\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"CallFromExcel\")\nusing CallFromExcel\nCallFromExcel.installme()\nPkg.add installs the Julia package, and installme() installs the Excel add-in.\nLaunch Excel. Check that it shows a Julia tab on the ribbon, and that typing =Julia into a worksheet cell makes the auto-complete offer JuliaCall, JuliaEval, JuliaInclude etc.\nAfter updating CallFromExcel (e.g. with Pkg.update()), close Excel and run using CallFromExcel; CallFromExcel.installme() again: the add-in and the Julia package must be the same version, and if they aren't, Excel says so. Do the same if the functions go missing from Excel.\nTo uninstall the Excel add-in, close Excel and find CallFromExcel in Windows' Settings > Apps > Installed apps. This also removes the ribbon's saved settings. The Julia package is separate: remove it with using Pkg; Pkg.rm(\"CallFromExcel\").","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#installation-linux","page":"Installation","text":"As an alternative to calling Julia running in a Windows process, CallFromExcel can call Julia running in a Linux process under Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL). If that's your preference, then in addition to the steps described above, you need to:\n\nIf necessary, install WSL with the default Linux distribution, Ubuntu.\nInstall Julia under WSL by running curl -fsSL https://install.julialang.org | sh at the Ubuntu prompt, per the official instructions. This installs juliaup, which puts julia on the PATH automatically.\nAt the Julia prompt (under WSL) install CallFromExcel by copy-pasting using Pkg; Pkg.add(\"CallFromExcel\") into the REPL.","title":"Installation (Linux)"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#upgrading-from-juliaexcel","page":"Installation","text":"CallFromExcel replaces JuliaExcel. Its worksheet functions have the same names, but some changes are needed to workbooks and code written for JuliaExcel.\n\nInstallation. Installing CallFromExcel (see above) also uninstalls JuliaExcel's Excel add-in, JuliaExcel.xlam. The JuliaExcel Julia package is no longer used, and can be removed with using Pkg; Pkg.rm(\"JuliaExcel\").\n\nWorksheet formulas. In a workbook saved with JuliaExcel, Excel records the location of JuliaExcel.xlam in each formula that uses its functions, so after upgrading a formula such as =JuliaLaunch() appears as ='C:\\Users\\Public\\JuliaExcel\\JuliaExcel.xlam'!JuliaLaunch(). Remove that path with Find and Replace (Ctrl+H): set \"Within\" to Workbook and \"Look in\" to Formulas, and replace the text 'C:\\Users\\Public\\JuliaExcel\\JuliaExcel.xlam'! with nothing.\n\nVBA.\n\nReplace the reference to JuliaExcel with one to CallFromExcel (VBA editor, Tools menu -> References).\nCreate an object to call CallFromExcel's functions through, and put it in front of each call, so JuliaCall(\"f\", x) becomes jl.JuliaCall(\"f\", x) - see Calling from VBA:\nDim jl As New CallFromExcel.ComServer\nFrom VBA, JuliaCall, JuliaCallVBA, JuliaCallNamedArgs and JuliaCallNamedArgsVBA take at most 30 arguments after the function name.\n\nJulia. Functions and variables formerly accessed as JuliaExcel.xxx, such as JuliaExcel.serve_xl() and JuliaExcel.last_answer, are now CallFromExcel.xxx, after using CallFromExcel.","title":"Upgrading from JuliaExcel"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#compatibility","page":"Installation","text":"CallFromExcel requires 64-bit Excel for Windows - the default for Microsoft 365 - and has been tested on Excel under Microsoft 365. It is not compatible with 32-bit Excel: the installer checks, and stops with an error if Excel is 32-bit. It requires Excel 2016 or later, and should work on 64-bit Excel 2016, 2019 and 2021, but has not been tested on them.","title":"Compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"ribbon/#The-Julia-ribbon-tab","page":"The Julia ribbon tab","text":"The add-in adds a Julia tab to Excel's ribbon:\n\n(Image: The Julia tab on Excel's ribbon, with one group, CallFromExcel.jl: Launch Julia, Launch Settings, Show Julia, Close Julia, Display Results, a Julia File box with Browse and Include, Register Functions, Help and About)\n\nControl What it does\nLaunch Julia Starts Julia for this Excel session, using the Launch Settings.\nLaunch Settings... Sets the project folder, other command line options, packages to load, whether to run under WSL, and so on. The settings are saved for you on this PC.\nShow Julia Brings Julia's window to the front.\nClose Julia Closes Julia, after asking for confirmation.\nDisplay Results Shows each call from Excel, and its result, in Julia's window - as CallFromExcel.display_results(true) does.\nFile, Browse, Include Choose a Julia file - typed, pasted, browsed to or picked from recently used files - and include it, as JuliaInclude does. Include doesn't trigger recalculation.\nRegister Functions Makes the Julia functions marked with @excel or expose_to_excel callable as worksheet functions - see Your own worksheet functions. That happens by itself after JuliaLaunch, JuliaInclude and JuliaEval; use the button for functions exposed from the Julia REPL.\nHelp Opens this documentation.\nAbout Shows the versions of the add-in, and of the CallFromExcel package and Julia in the Julia session it is connected to, and a link to this documentation.\n\nLaunch Julia and a JuliaLaunch formula start Julia in the same way, but each with its own settings: a formula uses only its own arguments, so a workbook records how Julia should start and does so the same way on every PC, while the ribbon's Launch Settings are yours.","title":"The Julia ribbon tab"},{"category":"section","location":"#CallFromExcel.jl-call-Julia-from-Excel","page":"Home","text":"Call Julia from Microsoft Excel worksheets and from VBA.\n\nCallFromExcel is an Excel add-in that lets you use any Julia function in a worksheet formula. For example, =JuliaEval(\"using LinearAlgebra\") makes the functions of one of Julia's standard libraries available, then =JuliaCall(\"eigvals\", A1:J10) passes a range to Julia as a matrix and returns its eigenvalues to the worksheet (as complex numbers, if the matrix isn't symmetric) - something Excel has no built-in function for. Keep your front end in Excel, and do the heavy computation in Julia.\n\nYour own Julia functions can also be worksheet functions in their own right: mark one with @excel and call it as =jl.my_function(A1, B1) - see Your own worksheet functions.\nKeep large or complex Julia objects - a big matrix, a fitted model, a DataFrame - in Julia, and pass them between formulas as handles: short strings such as jl:Array#17@k3x9 that stand for the objects.\nA Julia tab on Excel's ribbon starts Julia and loads your Julia files.\nCompatible with Excel's dynamic array formulas.\nSupports 64-bit Excel for Windows (not Mac, not 32-bit Excel).\nRun Julia under either Windows or Linux (via WSL).\nInstallation doesn't need admin rights.\nCallFromExcel is the successor to JuliaExcel, with its Excel add-in rewritten from VBA to C# for speed. See Upgrading from JuliaExcel.","title":"CallFromExcel.jl - call Julia from Excel"},{"category":"section","location":"#Demo","page":"Home","text":"Here's a quick demonstration of CallFromExcel's functions in action.\n\nSee how the Julia session on the left responds to the action in Excel on the right, once display_results(true) has been called (the formula at B6 does this).\nThe annotations in brown text (\"Formula at...\") are to make what's happening in the demo clearer. They won't appear when you try CallFromExcel for yourself!\nReplay the GIF by refreshing your browser (F5).\n\n(Image: Calling Julia from Excel worksheet formulas with JuliaLaunch, JuliaEval and JuliaCall, with the Julia REPL echoing each call)","title":"Demo"},{"category":"section","location":"how_it_works/#How-CallFromExcel-works","page":"How CallFromExcel works","text":"CallFromExcel's Excel add-in is written in C#, using Excel-DNA. Its worksheet functions are registered with Excel through the Excel SDK, and VBA calls the same code through the object created by CreateObject(\"CallFromExcel.ComServer\"). The Julia ribbon tab is part of the same add-in, and its buttons use the same code too. The add-in communicates with Julia over a local HTTP connection:\n\nJuliaLaunch starts a Julia process (as a normal Windows process, or under WSL) running a small HTTP server (CallFromExcel.start_server()) on a free local port, and writes that port number to a file so the add-in can find it. If a session for the current Excel process already appears to be running, JuliaLaunch checks that it's actually responding before reusing it, rather than starting a duplicate.\nJuliaEval POSTs the Julia expression as plain text to the server's /eval endpoint. The Julia function srv_eval_inner (in comms.jl) evaluates it with Meta.parseall/eval and returns the result - encoded in CallFromExcel's own wire format - in the HTTP response body.\nJuliaCall and JuliaCallVBA instead encode the function name and its arguments (marshalled per the rules described in Marshalling) into that same wire format and POST it to the /call endpoint. srv_call_inner decodes it and invokes the named function directly, returning the result the same way. This avoids Meta.parseing a literal expression, which is slow for calls passing large arrays. JuliaCallNamedArgs and JuliaCallNamedArgsVBA encode their arguments the same way but POST them to the /callkw endpoint, where Julia splits them at the \";\" into positional and named arguments.\nThe add-in decodes the response body into worksheet values or VBA variables.\n\nOther points to note:\n\nThe wire format distinguishes the types VBA does (e.g. Long from Double), and supports 2-D arrays (a worksheet range) and Excel error values. JSON, the obvious alternative, has only nested 1-D arrays and a much thinner type system. Arrays of numbers are sent in bulk, without per-element type or length information, and Julia ranges such as 1:1000000 are sent as just their first value, step and length.\nEach JuliaEval/JuliaCall is a synchronous HTTP request/response, over connections kept open between calls. If Julia isn't there to answer (e.g. after =JuliaEval(\"exit()\")), the call returns an error saying so.\nObject handles hook into Excel's RTD (real-time data) mechanism, through Excel-DNA's ExcelAsyncUtil.Observe. A JuliaCallHandle formula subscribes to an RTD topic keyed on the function name and its arguments, so the same formula implies the same object. Only the first formula to ask for a topic makes Julia build and store the object (CallFromExcel.handle_create) and return its handle. Later recalculations, and other formulas with the same function and arguments, get the same handle without calling Julia. When no formula uses a topic any more, Excel tells the add-in, which tells Julia to free the object. Any string argument matching the handle pattern is assumed to be a handle: it is sent to Julia as a reference, which Julia replaces with the stored object, or reports as not found. The key also includes a generation number, increased when JuliaLaunch finds a new Julia session, so that every handle formula subscribes again and rebuilds its object.\nRunning Julia inside Excel's own process, via Julia embedding, would avoid the HTTP round trip altogether. But Julia could then only be restarted by restarting Excel and there would be no separate Julia REPL to watch or debug in.","title":"How CallFromExcel works"},{"category":"section","location":"how_it_works/#Viewing-the-code","page":"How CallFromExcel works","text":"The Excel add-in is written in C#, using Excel-DNA; its source is in the dotnet-addin folder of the repository. Julia source code is always visible on your PC, and the @functionloc macro is an easy way to locate the code of any function you're interested in.","title":"Viewing the code"},{"category":"section","location":"handles/#Object-handles","page":"Object handles","text":"Some Julia objects are large, or have no sensible representation on a worksheet: a big matrix, a fitted model, a DataFrame, a dictionary. Rather than returning such an object to Excel, JuliaCallHandle (and JuliaEval with ReturnHandle TRUE) keeps it in Julia and returns a handle, a short string naming it, such as jl:Array#17@k3x9. Pass the handle to any function in place of the object, and Julia receives the object itself:\n\nCell Formula Result\nA1 =JuliaEval(\"rand(1000, 1000)\", TRUE) jl:Array#1@k3x9\nA2 =JuliaCall(\"sum\", A1) the sum of the matrix's million elements\nA3 =JuliaCallHandle(\"inv\", A1) jl:Array#2@k3x9, a handle to its inverse\nA4 =JuliaCall(\"maximum\", A3) the inverse's largest element\n\nThe matrix is built once and stays in Julia, however many formulas use it. A handle can be passed to JuliaCall, JuliaCallNamedArgs, JuliaCallHandle and their VBA counterparts, including inside a range or array: a range of handles arrives in Julia as a vector of the objects. =JuliaCall(\"identity\", A1) returns a handle's object to the worksheet as data. A handle's text is jl:<label>#<id>@<session>: the label is the object's type (or a label you choose, see below), for people reading the sheet, the id numbers each object, and the session identifies the Julia process holding it.\n\nPoints to note:\n\nLifetime. A handle's object stays in Julia while a formula that creates it exists. Edit or delete that formula, or change its inputs, and the object is freed (and a new one created for new inputs). Excel sometimes frees an object only at a later recalculation, for example after a workbook is closed.\nSame formula, same object. Formulas with the same function and the same arguments share one object, as do copies of a cell, and recalculating - even with Ctrl+Alt+F9 - doesn't rebuild it. So =JuliaEval(\"rand(3)\", TRUE) keeps its first random numbers. To get a separate, or new, object, change an input, for example an extra argument that the Julia function ignores. Running the macro JuliaHandleInvalidateAll (Alt+F8, type the name, Run) rebuilds every handle's object.\nVolatile arguments. A handle formula whose arguments change at every recalculation - because it uses a volatile function such as RAND, RANDARRAY, NOW, OFFSET or INDIRECT, directly or through the cells it refers to - can't keep a handle. In automatic calculation, each new handle makes Excel recalculate its cell, which then has new arguments, so another handle is created, and so on. CallFromExcel notices after a few rounds and returns an error saying the arguments change at every recalculation. Instead:\ngenerate the data in Julia, e.g. =JuliaEval(\"rand(1000)\", TRUE) rather than =JuliaCallHandle(\"identity\", RANDARRAY(1000));\nif the result needn't stay in Julia, use JuliaCall, e.g. =JuliaCall(\"sum\", RANDARRAY(1000));\ncopy the volatile values and paste them as values;\nor, to get a new object at every recalculation (F9), switch to manual calculation (Formulas > Calculation Options > Manual), where Excel doesn't recalculate after each new handle. Note that this setting applies to all open workbooks.\nDon't change a handle's object. A function that changes its argument, such as push! or sort!, changes the stored object without changing its handle, so cells using it aren't recalculated, and results depend on the order Excel calculates in. Use functions that return a new object, such as sort, and make a new handle of the result.\nWhen Julia restarts. JuliaLaunch, and the ribbon's Launch Julia button, notice a new Julia session and rebuild every handle's object in it, in the order Excel's dependencies require. A cell that defines functions the handles need should refer to the launch cell, so that it is recalculated first, e.g. =IF(LEN(Z1)>0, JuliaInclude(\"C:\\MyCode\\models.jl\")) where Z1 holds =JuliaLaunch(). Until then - for example after Julia is restarted from VBA - a handle from the old session gives an error saying it was not found.\nStrings that look like handles are handles. Any string matching the pattern jl:<label>#<id>@<session> is taken to be a handle, whether a formula returned it or it was typed. If its object has been freed, or it comes from an earlier Julia session (for example, saved in a workbook), the call gives a \"not found\" error, rather than passing the string to Julia.\nErrors pass through. If an argument to JuliaCallHandle, or to a function exposed with handle=true, is an error value, such as #N/A, it returns that error without calling Julia. (JuliaCall instead passes the error to Julia, as an ExcelError.) This is because a handle cell shows #N/A while its object is being built, and after Julia restarts until it is rebuilt: in a chain of handles, each cell waits for the one before it, rather than running its function on #N/A and creating an object that is replaced moments later. An error inside an array argument, such as a range containing #N/A, is passed to Julia as usual.\nFrom VBA. VBA can pass handles from cells to jl.JuliaCall etc. Handles that VBA creates, with jl.JuliaCallHandle or jl.JuliaEvalHandle, belong to no formula, so they last until jl.JuliaHandleRelease or jl.JuliaHandleReleaseAll frees them, or Julia exits. (Calling the worksheet function through Application.Run or Evaluate gives a handle that is freed straight away.)\nLabels for your own types. The label is the name of the object's type, without its module or parameters (Array for a Matrix{Float64}). To choose your own, add a method to CallFromExcel.handle_label, for example CallFromExcel.handle_label(x::YieldCurve) = \"Curve_$(x.currency)\", so that a sheet shows jl:Curve_USD#3@k3x9. The label is worked out once, when the handle is created, and any #, @, whitespace or control character in it is replaced by _.\n\nThe functions for handles are JuliaCallHandle, JuliaEvalHandle, JuliaHandleRelease, JuliaHandleReleaseAll and JuliaHandleInfo, and JuliaEval with ReturnHandle TRUE.","title":"Object handles"}]
}
